"Canada and Ukraine to scale up drone production, build up Canada’s defence industries, and achieve a just and lasting peace in Ukraine | Prime Minister of Canada (https://www.pm.gc.ca/en/news/backgrounders/2026/09/10/canada-and-ukraine-scale-drone-production-build-canadas-defence)\nciteturn17view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.pm.gc.ca/en/news/backgrounders/2026/09/10/canada-and-ukraine-scale-drone-production-build-canadas-defence\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 149\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: \nL2: cite1†Prime Minister of Canada L3: \nL4:   * News\nL5:     * cite2†All News L6:     * cite3†News Releases L7:     * cite4†Statements L8:     * cite5†Readouts L9:     * cite6†Speeches L10:     * cite7†Media Advisories L11:   * cite8†About L12:   * The Ministry\nL13:     * cite9†Cabinet L14:     * cite10†Secretaries of State L15:     * cite11†Parliamentary Secretaries L16:     * cite12†Committees L17:     * cite13†Mandate Letter L18:     * cite14†Open Government L19:   * cite15†Connect L20:   * Media\nL21:     * cite16†Photos L22:     * cite17†Videos L23: \nL24: Menu\nL25: \nL26: cite1†Prime Minister of Canada L27:   * News\nL28:     * cite2†All News L29:     * cite3†News Releases L30:     * cite4†Statements L31:     * cite5†Readouts L32:     * cite6†Speeches L33:     * cite7†Media Advisories L34:   * cite8†About L35:   * The Ministry\nL36:     * cite9†Cabinet L37:     * cite10†Secretaries of State L38:     * cite11†Parliamentary Secretaries L39:     * cite12†Committees L40:     * cite13†Mandate Letter L41:     * cite14†Open Government L42:   * cite15†Connect L43:   * Media\nL44:     * cite16†Photos L45:     * cite17†Videos L46:   * cite18†Privacy Statement L47:   * cite19†Ownership & Usage L48:   * cite20†Social Media Use L49:   * cite21†Help L50: \nL51: cite22†Image: Search L52: cite23†Image: Prime Minister Crest L53: # Canada and Ukraine to scale up drone production, build up Canada’s defence industries, and achieve a just and lasting peace in Ukraine\nL54: \nL55: Type keyword(s)\nL56: \nL57: [Input: Type keyword(s)]Search\nL58: \nL59: Enter the terms you wish to search for.\nL60: \nL61: [Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input]\nL62: ## Share this Page\nL63: \nL64: September 10, 2026\nL65: \nL66: Calgary, Alberta\nL67: \nL68: This week, the Prime Minister, Mark Carney, welcomed the President of Ukraine, Volodymyr Zelenskyy, to Canada to forge a new chapter in the Canada-Ukraine partnership. In Calgary, Prime Minister Carney announced the establishment of a national drone marketplace to accelerate Canada’s own production capabilities.\nL69: \nL70: ## Scaling up Canada’s defence industry\nL71: ### Defence Drone Initiative Marketplace Supply Arrangement\nL72: Canada has successfully established the Defence Drone Initiative Marketplace (DDIM) Supply Arrangement. Modelled after Ukraine’s world-leading Brave1, this national drone marketplace will ensure the reliable, rapid, and continuous supply of drones for the Canadian Armed Forces and Coast Guard. It will connect operators and end-users to this Canadian defence ecosystem to accelerate the discovery, acquisition, and fielding of uncrewed and counter-uncrewed capabilities.\nL73: To date, nearly 400 vendors have qualified, and up to $50 million in initial contracts have been awarded to Canadian DDIM suppliers. This initial batch of contracts, focused on low-cost uncrewed aircraft systems (UAS) and complex terrain uncrewed ground vehicles (UGV), will introduce multiple new platforms to the Canadian Armed Forces (CAF) and increase its inventory tenfold.\nL74: It will also strengthen Canada’s drone industrial ecosystem by positioning it to manufacture, at scale, these systems which are now vital in modern warfare. Another competition for uncrewed vehicles supporting maritime mine-countermeasure operations will be launched imminently, helping develop world-leading drone technology right here in Canada.\nL75: Further requests for proposals will be published under the DDIM within the coming weeks and months – providing the CAF with the capabilities that it needs, while building a strong and dynamic drone industry to help Canada better support its partners, including Ukraine.\nL76: Following the completion of the evaluation process, including Foreign Ownership, Control and Influence (FOCI) assessments, Canada intends to proceed with contract awards to the following suppliers:\nL77: #### Uncrewed aircraft systems (UAS)\nL78: \nL79: Vendor: AVSS\nL80: System: AVSS Drone D1\nL81: Locations: Fredericton, New Brunswick, and Carp, Ontario\nL82: The AVSS Drone D1 is a predominantly Canadian-made system. Manufacturing and assembly take place in Ontario. The majority of major components, software, electronics, sensors, and support systems are sourced from Canada, with limited content sourced from allied countries, including the United States, France, and the Netherlands.\nL83: Vendor: Volatus Aerospace\nL84: System: Huntsman X6\nL85: Location: Canadian operations\nL86: The Huntsman X6 is a United Kingdom (UK)-manufactured platform with Canadian support and sustainment. Volatus provides Canadian contract management, logistics, customer support, training, and lifecycle sustainment services, ensuring continuing Canadian economic and operational benefits.\nL87: Vendor: Draganfly\nL88: System: Astiia UAS\nL89: Location: Saskatoon, Saskatchewan\nL90: Draganfly is a Saskatchewan-based Canadian aerospace company with established Canadian engineering, manufacturing, integration, training, and sustainment capabilities. The proposed platform is based on proven Australian technology, with Canadian production under licence and manufacturing, integration, support, training, lifecycle sustainment, technology transfer, and ongoing product development taking place in Canada.\nL91: Vendor: Objexis AI\nL92: System: MAQ-C\nL93: Location: Oakville, Ontario\nL94: The MAQ-C is a Canadian-designed and Canadian-built platform, with Canadian-owned intellectual property, software, mission systems, flight-control architecture, ATAK integration, additive manufacturing, and system integration conducted in Canada. The majority of software, avionics, command-and-control systems, and integration work is performed domestically, with critical components sourced from Canada and allied nations.\nL95: #### Uncrewed ground vehicles (UGV)\nL96: \nL97: Vendor: Twenty20 Insight Inc.\nL98: System: XRC RHINO\nL99: Location: Ottawa, Ontario\nL100: The XRC RHINO platform is designed and manufactured in the UK, with Canadian service delivery. Twenty20 Insight Inc. provides Canadian contract management, systems engineering, training, documentation, customer support, configuration management, and sustainment activities.\nL101: Vendor: Beonyx\nL102: System: VTSE-TC\nL103: Location: Saguenay, Québec\nL104: VTSE-TC is a Canadian-designed, Canadian-owned, and Canadian-manufactured system. Intellectual property, engineering, support, assembly, and manufacturing are conducted in Canada, primarily in Québec, representing a fully Canadian UGV solution.\nL105: \nL106: ***\nL107: Deepening our long-standing partnership and reaffirming Canada’s support for Ukraine in the face of Russia’s ongoing war of aggression, Prime Minister Carney announced additional investments and initiatives in defence, humanitarian aid, energy infrastructure, and support for Ukraine’s ongoing security and economic resilience.\nL108: ## Military assistance\nL109: Support for participation in JUMPSTART\nL110: Partner: Joint Ukraine Multinational Program – Services, Training and Articles Rapid Timeline (JUMPSTART)\nL111: Funding: Approximately $350 million\nL112: The sustained delivery of air defence equipment remains critical to Ukraine’s ability to protect its people and critical infrastructure against Russia’s escalated aerial attacks. In response to this priority requirement, Canada, alongside its Allies, is providing funding to the United States’ JUMPSTART mechanism to help meet this operational need and keep Ukrainians safe.\nL113: ## Support for Ukrainian children\nL114: Supporting the return, rehabilitation, and reintegration of Ukrainian children forcibly transferred and unlawfully deported by Russia\nL115: Funding: Up to $4 million\nL116: This funding will support the work of organisations and government partners advancing the return and reintegration of Ukrainian children who have been forcibly transferred and unlawfully deported by Russia. Canada is helping Ukraine strengthen the systems that make return and reintegration possible: from tracing and verification, to return, rehabilitation, reintegration, and community-based services.\nL117: Supporting Ukraine’s State Reintegration Service\nL118: Partner: UNICEF\nL119: Funding: Additional $2 million, for a total of $12 million\nL120: This funding supports UNICEF’s work in Ukraine to pilot its State Reintegration Service project in 15 communities. This state service will help establish a nationally defined pathway for returned children, connecting them and their families with the services they need for rehabilitation and reintegration.\nL121: ## Rebuilding critical energy infrastructure\nL122: \nL123: European Bank for Reconstruction and Development (EBRD) loan guarantee\nL124: Funding: Approximately $435 million\nL125: Canada is contributing an additional loan guarantee to the EBRD for Naftogaz, which will help Ukraine’s energy security including by financing winter gas purchases.\nL126: Contribution to the Ukraine Energy Support Fund\nL127: Funding: Additional $10 million for a total of $100 million\nL128: The Ukraine Energy Support Fund (UESF) is a multi-donor platform established in 2022 to counteract the impact of Russian attacks against critical energy infrastructure. Canada is contributing further funding to the UESF to procure and deliver energy equipment for restoring damaged critical energy infrastructure, maintain essential energy services, and strengthen Ukraine’s energy resilience.\nL129: Supporting the International Energy Agency (IEA) Joint Work Programme\nL130: Funding: $2 million\nL131: This funding will strengthen Ukraine’s energy resilience, provide regulatory support, and advance clean energy projects.\nL132: ## Deterring threats and promoting long-term social, economic, and security resilience\nL133: Multi-donor pooled initiative to support Ukraine’s veterans and their families\nL134: Funding: Up to $40.1 million\nL135: Canada and Ukraine are developing a multi-donor pooled initiative to support the implementation of Ukraine’s National Veteran Strategy, thereby providing support to Ukrainian veterans and their families.\nL136: This initiative will further four broad areas of work: health, rehabilitation, and recovery; access to services and benefits; economic opportunity and livelihoods; and social inclusion and community participation for veterans and their families. Through this initiative, Canada will bring together governments, civil society organisations, and other stakeholders to foster coordination, share expertise and lessons learned as well as strengthen Ukraine’s long-term capacity to respond to the evolving needs of veterans.\nL137: Canada and Ukraine are inviting interested partner countries to also contribute to this collective endeavour.\nL138: Investing in Ukraine’s long-term security and resilience to hybrid threats\nL139: Funding: $22,746,180\nL140: Canada is investing in Ukraine’s long-term security, stability, and resilience by supporting the capabilities of Ukraine’s security architecture to better detect, prevent, and respond to complex and hybrid threats. This funding includes support to increase national capacities to manage weapons, secure borders, and maintain stability in liberated territories. It will also help counter hybrid threats through investments in cybersecurity, critical infrastructure protection, and disinformation response.\nL141: Thirdly, it will support civilian safety by strengthening coordination between security forces and frontline communities affected by Russia’s aggression.\nL142: Concessional financing envelope to support the reconstruction of Ukraine’s critical infrastructure\nL143: Funding: $200 million\nL144: Canada is dedicating $200 million of a $2 billion concessional financing envelope administered by Export Development Canada to support the reconstruction of Ukraine’s critical infrastructure. The Government of Canada intends for support under this allocation to be directed first toward a hydroelectric infrastructure opportunity in Ukraine that responds to urgent energy needs while contributing to the country’s reconstruction.\nL145: The proposed support would allow Canadian expertise to contribute to technical planning and engineering work that could improve grid flexibility, support frequency regulation, and provide additional emergency reserves for Ukraine’s electricity system.\nL146: ## Related product\nL147: \nL148:   * cite24†Canada and Ukraine to scale up drone production, build up Canada’s defence industries, and achieve a just and lasting peace in Ukraine --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDefence Drone Initiative - Canada.ca (https://www.canada.ca/en/department-national-defence/news/2026/07/defence-drone-initiative.html)\nciteturn17view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.canada.ca/en/department-national-defence/news/2026/07/defence-drone-initiative.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 133\nL0:   * cite0†Skip to main content L1:   * cite1†Skip to \"About government\" L2: \nL3: ## Language selection\nL4: \nL5:   * cite2†fr (Français) L6: \nL7: cite3†/ Gouvernement du Canada L8: \nL9: ## Search\nL10: \nL11: Search National Defence [Input: Search National Defence]\nL12: \nL13: Search\nL14: \nL15: * * *\nL16: \nL17: ## Menu\nL18: \nL19: Main Menu\nL20: \nL21: ## You are here:\nL22: \nL23: # Defence Drone Initiative\nL24: \nL25: From: cite4†National Defence L26: ## Backgrounder\nL27: \nL28: The Government of Canada launched the Defence Drone Initiative (DDI) to accelerate the development, testing, adaptation, and production of uncrewed and autonomous systems for the Canadian Armed Forces (CAF) and the Canadian Coast Guard (CCG).\nL29: \nL30: July 23, 2026 – Ottawa, Ontario – National Defence / Canadian Armed Forces\nL31: ## Overview\nL32: The Government of Canada launched the Defence Drone Initiative (DDI) to accelerate the development, testing, adaptation, and production of uncrewed and autonomous systems for the Canadian Armed Forces (CAF) and the Canadian Coast Guard (CCG).\nL33: Led collaboratively by the Department of National Defence and the Defence Investment Agency (DIA), the initiative will establish a competitive supply arrangement that connects operational requirements with Canadian innovation and creates a more agile pathway to develop, acquire and integrate these technologies.\nL34: Recent conflicts have shown that drone technology evolves rapidly. Canada has world-class expertise across aerospace, robotics, artificial intelligence, advanced manufacturing, sensors, secure communications, propulsion and advanced materials but these capabilities are distributed across industry, academia and government.\nL35: The DDI will help build a more responsive and scalable Canadian ecosystem and it reflects the Government of Canada's commitment to provide the CAF and CCG with access to Canadian-developed technologies that enhance operational readiness while supporting Canadian innovation, skilled jobs and long-term economic growth.\nL36: ## Initial priority areas\nL37: \nL38: Six initial use cases have been identified to provide focus for industry engagement and capability development.\nL39:   * Low-cost tactical intelligence, surveillance and reconnaissance drones\nL40: Small, affordable uncrewed aircraft could provide tactical units with local intelligence, surveillance and reconnaissance capabilities.\nL41: The objective is to identify secure and affordable systems that can be made widely available to operators, tested in the field and adapted based on user feedback.\nL42:   * Uncrewed ground vehicles for logistics and complex terrain\nL43: Ground-based robotic systems could help carry equipment, batteries, water and other supplies across difficult or dangerous terrain.\nL44: Potential applications could also include casualty support, communications relay and operations in environments where sending personnel may present additional risk.\nL45:   * Uncrewed maritime surface and underwater systems\nL46: Maritime systems could support route surveys, the detection and classification of mine-like objects, maritime awareness and operations in constrained waters.\nL47: These technologies may also help support Arctic and coastal surveillance, port security and the protection of critical maritime routes.\nL48:   * Standardized drone munitions and explosive payloads\nL49: The initiative will examine options for standardized and properly certified payloads, interfaces and safety systems for military uncrewed platforms.\nL50: A common approach could reduce duplication, improve interoperability and support safer integration across different systems.\nL51:   * Low-cost, low collateral counter-drone interceptors\nL52: The initiative will explore affordable systems that could detect, track or defeat hostile drones.\nL53: This includes potential non-explosive interceptors and other low-collateral options that could contribute to the protection of military personnel, installations and infrastructure.\nL54:   * Deep precision strike systems\nL55: The initiative will examine technologies that could contribute to a sovereign Canadian long-range precision capability.\nL56: This is a more complex area that will require additional technical, operational, policy, legal, safety and security work. No acquisition decision is being announced at this stage.\nL57: ## Competitive supplier process\nL58: \nL59: A Request for Supply Arrangement (RFSA) will establish a pool of Canadian qualified suppliers across multiple areas of uncrewed and autonomous systems. Applicants will be assessed against the requirements set out in the RFSA. Qualification will not guarantee a contract but may allow successful suppliers to compete for future work, including research, prototyping, testing, demonstrations, limited acquisitions and potential production opportunities.\nL60:   * The process is intended to engage a broad range of organizations, including start-ups, small and medium-sized enterprises, established manufacturers, defence and aerospace firms, universities, colleges, research organizations and Indigenous businesses.\nL61:   * Previous defence experience is not necessarily required.\nL62:   * Relevant technical experience, security readiness and the ability to meet operational requirements will be assessed through the formal process.\nL63: The RFSA is open until August 14, 2026. To support interested suppliers, the DIA will host a virtual information session on July 30, 2026, to provide additional information on the RFSA and the submission process. Representatives from the Canadian Joint Forces Command will be present to answer any questions on capability requirements.\nL64:   * Participation in earlier outreach activities is not required to apply.\nL65:   * All formal procurement requirements and deadlines will be made available through CanadaBuys, the Government of Canada’s official electronic tendering service\nL66: ## Why Canada is acting\nL67:   * Canadian sovereign capability\nL68: The initiative aims to strengthen Canada's ability to access, operate, sustain, adapt and scale critical uncrewed and autonomous systems. Depending on the capability, this may include domestic development and production, licensed manufacturing, partnerships with trusted allies, protection of intellectual property and more secure supply chains.\nL69: \nL70:   * Security and supply chains\nL71: Security considerations will be integrated throughout the initiative, including cybersecurity, supply-chain integrity, ownership and control, personnel security and the sourcing of sensitive technologies.\nL72:   * Economic and industrial benefits\nL73: The initiative is expected to support innovation, skilled jobs and domestic industrial capacity in areas such as artificial intelligence, robotics, aerospace, advanced manufacturing, secure communications, sensors, propulsion, software and advanced materials.\nL74:   * Supporting Canada and its Allies\nL75: The initiative’s primary objective is to deliver operationally relevant capabilities to the Canadian Armed Forces and the Canadian Coast Guard. In an increasingly complex and unpredictable security environment, the CAF and the CCG must be able to operate effectively across all domains, including land, air, sea, cyber, and space, alongside allies and partners, and at speed.\nL76: This initiative will help modernize and strengthen those capabilities to meet evolving operational challenges at home and abroad while contributing to collective defence, supporting alliance commitments, and helping promote international security and stability.\nL77: ## Contacts\nL78: \nL79: Media Relations\nL80: \nL81: Department of National Defence\nL82: Phone: 613-904-3333\nL83: Email: mlo-blm@forces.gc.ca\nL84: \nL85: Search for related information by keyword: cite5†Military | cite6†National Defence | cite7†Canada | cite8†National security and defence | cite9†general public | cite10†backgrounders L86: \nL87: ## Page details\nL88: \nL89: 2026-07-23\nL90: \nL91: ## About this site\nL92: \nL93: ### National Defence\nL94: \nL95:   * cite11†Contact us L96:   * cite12†News L97:   * cite13†Jobs L98: \nL99: ### Government of Canada\nL100: \nL101:   * cite14†All contacts L102:   * cite15†Departments and agencies L103:   * cite16†About government L104: #### Themes and topics\nL105:   * cite17†Jobs L106:   * cite18†Immigration and citizenship L107:   * cite19†Travel and tourism†travel.gc.ca L108:   * cite20†Business L109:   * cite21†Benefits L110:   * cite22†Health L111:   * cite23†Taxes L112:   * cite24†Environment and natural resources L113:   * cite25†National security and defence L114:   * cite26†Culture, history and sport L115:   * cite27†Policing, justice and emergencies L116:   * cite28†Transport and infrastructure L117:   * cite29†Canada and the world†www.international.gc.ca L118:   * cite30†Money and finances L119:   * cite31†Science and innovation L120:   * cite32†Indigenous Peoples L121:   * cite33†Veterans and military L122:   * cite34†Youth L123:   * cite35†Manage life events L124: ### Government of Canada Corporate\nL125: \nL126:   * cite36†Social media L127:   * cite37†Mobile applications L128:   * cite38†About Canada.ca L129:   * cite39†Terms and conditions L130:   * cite40†Privacy L131: \nL132: cite41†Image: Symbol of the Government of Canada --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nwww.sec.gov (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1786286/000149315226007532/ex99-1.htm)\nciteturn17view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1786286/000149315226007532/ex99-1.htm\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 85\nL0: EX-99.1 2 ex99-1.htm EX-99.1\nL1: \nL2: \nL3: \nL4: Exhibit 99.1\nL5: \nL6: \nL7: \nL8: cite0†Image L9: \nL10: \nL11: \nL12: Draganfly Welcomes Lieutenant-General (Ret’d) Michel Gauthier to Military Advisory Board in Support of Canada’s Defence Industrial Strategy\nL13: \nL14: \nL15: \nL16: Ottawa, ON — February 20, 2026— Draganfly Inc. (NASDAQ: DPRO; CSE: DPRO; FSE: 3U8A) (“Draganfly” or the “Company”), an award-winning developer of drone solutions, software, and robotics, today announced the appointment of Lieutenant-General (Ret’d) Michel Gauthier to its Military Advisory Board.\nL17: The appointment comes as Canada advances its renewed Defence Industrial Strategy, a national framework focused on strengthening sovereign defence production, accelerating innovation, modernizing procurement, and reducing reliance on foreign supply chains.\nL18: Lieutenant-General Gauthier brings more than 36 years of distinguished service with the Canadian Armed Forces. He served as Commander of the Canadian Expeditionary Force Command (CEFCOM), where he was responsible for operational command of Canadian Forces deployed internationally, including during Canada’s mission in Afghanistan. In that role, he oversaw strategic planning, coalition coordination, and mission execution across complex theatres of operation.\nL19: Since retiring from active service, Lt-Gen (Ret’d) Gauthier has remained closely engaged in defence, security, and public policy advisory work, including senior roles in government relations and strategic advisory services. His experience bridges military leadership, defence policy, and public-private collaboration, areas that are central to Canada’s evolving industrial defence priorities.\nL20: “We are honoured to welcome Lieutenant-General Gauthier to Draganfly’s Advisory Board,” said Cameron Chell, Executive Chairman and Chief Executive Officer of Draganfly. “His distinguished military leadership, deep understanding of Canada’s defence architecture, and proven expertise in government and international engagement will be invaluable as we work with Canada in its newly announced and rapidly expanding Defence Industrial Strategy.\nL21: His decision to join Draganfly represents a powerful strategic endorsement of our technology, our mission, and our growing role within the sovereign defence ecosystem.”\nL22: \nL23: \nL24: Canada’s renewed defence industrial focus emphasizes expanded domestic production capacity, accelerated R&D investment, resilient supply chains, and support for advanced technologies including AI-enabled systems, robotics, and autonomous platforms. Draganfly, as an unmanned aerial system and technology platform manufacturer, is positioned to contribute meaningfully to these national objectives.\nL25: The Company continues to expand its engagement within defence and government markets through manufacturing, advisory leadership, and product innovation designed to meet the operational needs of modern military environments.\nL26: \nL27: \nL28: \nL29: \nL30: \nL31: \nL32: \nL33: \nL34: \nL35: About Draganfly\nL36: Draganfly Inc. (NASDAQ: DPRO; CSE: DPRO; FSE: 3U8A) is a leader in cutting-edge drone solutions and software that are transforming industries and serving stakeholders globally. Recognized for innovation and excellence for over 25 years, Draganfly delivers award-winning technology to the public safety, agriculture, industrial inspection, security, mapping, and surveying markets.\nL37: The Company is driven by passion, ingenuity, and a mission to provide efficient solutions and first-class services to customers worldwide, saving time, money, and lives.\nL38: \nL39: \nL40: For more information, visit www.draganfly.com.\nL41: \nL42: \nL43: \nL44: For investor details, visit:\nL45: \nL46: \nL47: \nL48: NASDAQ (DPRO)\nL49: \nL50: CSE (DPRO)\nL51: \nL52: FSE (3U8A)\nL53: \nL54: \nL55: \nL56: Media Contact\nL57: \nL58: \nL59: \nL60: Erika Racicot\nL61: Email: media@draganfly.com\nL62: \nL63: \nL64: \nL65: Company Contact\nL66: \nL67: \nL68: \nL69: Cameron Chell\nL70: \nL71: Chief Executive Officer\nL72: \nL73: (306) 955-9907\nL74: \nL75: info@draganfly.com\nL76: \nL77: \nL78: \nL79: Forward-Looking Statements\nL80: This release contains certain “forward looking statements” and certain “forward-looking ‎‎‎‎information” as ‎‎‎‎defined under applicable securities laws. Forward-looking statements ‎‎‎‎and information can ‎‎‎‎generally be identified by the use of forward-looking terminology such as ‎‎‎‎‎“may”, “will”, “expect”, “intend”, ‎‎‎‎‎“estimate”, “anticipate”, “believe”, “continue”, “plans” or similar ‎‎‎‎terminology.\nL81: Forward-looking statements ‎‎‎‎and information are based on forecasts of future ‎‎‎‎results, estimates of amounts not yet determinable and ‎‎‎‎assumptions that, while believed by ‎‎‎‎management to be reasonable, are inherently subject to significant ‎‎‎‎business, economic and ‎‎‎‎competitive uncertainties and contingencies.\nL82: Forward-looking statements include, but are not limited to, statements with respect to Draganfly’s anticipated role within Canada’s Defence Industrial Strategy; the Company’s positioning within sovereign defence initiatives; its expected engagement and expansion within defence and government markets; the strategic impact of the appointment of Lieutenant-General (Ret’d) Michel Gauthier to the Advisory Board; and the Company’s ability to contribute to domestic production capacity, innovation, and advanced drone, robotics, and autonomous systems initiatives.\nL83: Forward-‎‎‎‎looking statements and information are subject to various ‎known ‎‎and unknown risks and ‎‎‎‎‎uncertainties, many of which are beyond the ability of the Company to ‎control or ‎‎predict, that ‎‎‎‎may cause ‎the Company’s actual results, performance or achievements to be ‎materially ‎‎different ‎‎‎‎from those ‎expressed or implied thereby, and are developed based on assumptions ‎about ‎‎such ‎‎‎‎risks, uncertainties ‎and other factors set out here in, including but not limited to: the potential ‎‎‎‎‎‎‎impact of epidemics, ‎pandemics or other public health crises, including the ‎COVID-19 pandemic, on the Company’s business, operations and financial ‎‎‎‎condition; the ‎‎‎successful integration of ‎technology; the inherent risks involved in the ge